* The standard BrainFuck idioms, named.
*
* Every one of these is in the catalogue at
* https://esolangs.org/wiki/Brainfuck_algorithms, written there against cells
* called x, y and temp0 -- because prose has no other way to talk about a cell.
* Here they take the cells as parameters, so `@copy(count, scratch, spare)`
* says which three, and the expander emits the movement between them.
*
* This file declares and does not emit. It includes another that does the same.
*
* Every macro below carries the same thing above it: what it would be if the
* language had a way to say it. BrainFuck reads a cell at a time and an
* algorithm does not, so the loops are the letters and the block in the
* comment is the word.

@include "ascii.bfm"

* Empty a cell. `[+]` also gets there, in about 256 iterations.
*
*     cell = 0
@macro clear(cell) {
    @to cell
    [-]
}

* A cell holding a value, whatever it held before.
*
*     cell = value
@macro put(cell, value) {
    @clear(cell)
    @to cell
    +{value}
}

* `to` gains what `from` had; `from` is left empty. The loop every other idiom
* here is built out of.
*
*     while from:
*         to += 1
*         from -= 1
@macro move(from, to) {
    @to from
    [
        @to to
        +
        @to from
        -
    ]
}

* `to` gains what `from` has, and `from` keeps it. A cell can only be read by
* emptying it, so the value goes to both `to` and `scratch` and comes back from
* `scratch` -- which is why copying needs a cell it is allowed to destroy.
*
*     scratch = 0
*     while from:
*         to += 1
*         scratch += 1
*         from -= 1
*     while scratch:            # and put it back
*         from += 1
*         scratch -= 1
@macro copy(from, to, scratch) {
    @clear(scratch)
    @to from
    [
        @to to
        +
        @to scratch
        +
        @to from
        -
    ]
    @move(scratch, from)
}

* `flag` becomes 1 if the cell holds anything, 0 if it is empty. The question
* BrainFuck can ask directly -- `[` asks it -- written as a value, so that a
* program can ask it about a cell it is not standing on and act on the answer
* later.
*
*     flag = 0
*     scratch = copy(cell)
*     while scratch:            # runs once, or not at all
*         flag = 1
*         scratch = 0
@macro is_not_zero(cell, flag, scratch, spare) {
    @clear(flag)
    @clear(scratch)
    @copy(cell, scratch, spare)
    @to scratch
    [
        @put(flag, 1)
        @clear(scratch)
    ]
}

* And the other way round.
*
*     flag = 1
*     scratch = copy(cell)
*     while scratch:
*         flag = 0
*         scratch = 0
@macro is_zero(cell, flag, scratch, spare) {
    @put(flag, 1)
    @clear(scratch)
    @copy(cell, scratch, spare)
    @to scratch
    [
        @clear(flag)
        @clear(scratch)
    ]
}

* `x` becomes `x` times `y`; `y` keeps its value. Both scratch cells are left
* empty.
*
* Adding `y` to nothing, `x` times over. That is what multiplication is when
* the only arithmetic there is is adding one -- and why it costs the product
* rather than the number of digits, which is the thing to know before using it
* on anything large.
*
*     temp1 = x
*     x = 0
*     while temp1:
*         x += y
*         temp1 -= 1
@macro multiply(x, y, temp0, temp1) {
    @clear(temp0)
    @clear(temp1)
    @move(x, temp1)
    @to temp1
    [
        @copy(y, x, temp0)
        @to temp1
        -
    ]
}

* `x` becomes 1 if it equalled `y` and 0 if it did not; `y` keeps its value.
*
* Counting one down against the other and seeing whether anything is left,
* which is the only way to compare two cells without a subtraction that can go
* below zero.
*
*     temp1 = x
*     x = 1
*     temp1 -= y               # y is copied out and put back as it goes
*     if temp1:                # anything left over means they differed
*         x = 0
@macro equal(x, y, temp0, temp1) {
    @clear(temp0)
    @clear(temp1)
    @move(x, temp1)
    @to x
    +
    @to y
    [
        @to temp1
        -
        @to temp0
        +
        @to y
        -
    ]
    @move(temp0, y)
    @to temp1
    [
        @clear(x)
        @clear(temp1)
    ]
}

* `result` becomes 1 if `x` was less than `y`, and 0 if it was not. Both `x`
* and `y` keep their values; the answer is in `result`, which is the one thing
* here that does not answer in its first operand.
*
* Counts them down together and sees which runs out first. `passed` remembers
* that `y` ran out, which is the case that says `x` was not smaller.
*
*     result = 0
*     count, step, passed = x, y, 0
*     while count:
*         if step == 0:
*             passed = 1       # y ran out first: x is not smaller
*         else:
*             step -= 1
*         count -= 1
*     if not passed and step:  # x ran out and y had further to go
*         result = 1
@macro less(x, y, result, count, step, passed, flag, other, scratch, spare) {
    @clear(result)
    @clear(count)
    @copy(x, count, spare)
    @clear(step)
    @copy(y, step, spare)
    @clear(passed)
    @to count
    [
        @is_zero(step, flag, scratch, spare)
        @to flag
        [
            @put(passed, 1)
            @clear(flag)
        ]
        @is_not_zero(step, flag, scratch, spare)
        @to flag
        [
            @to step
            -
            @clear(flag)
        ]
        @to count
        -
    ]
    @is_zero(passed, flag, scratch, spare)
    @to flag
    [
        @is_not_zero(step, other, scratch, spare)
        @to other
        [
            @put(result, 1)
            @clear(other)
        ]
        @clear(flag)
    ]
}

* The two cells trade values.
*
*     temp0, x, y = x, y, temp0
@macro swap(x, y, temp0) {
    @clear(temp0)
    @move(x, temp0)
    @move(y, x)
    @move(temp0, y)
}

* Run the body until the cell is empty. The loop BrainFuck has, with the
* bookkeeping that makes it safe written once instead of at every use.
*
*     while cell:
*         body
*
* The `@to cell` at the end of the body is the whole reason this is worth
* naming: a loop must leave the cursor where it found it, and forgetting that
* is the mistake every hand-written loop in this corpus had to avoid.
@macro while(cell, body) {
    @to cell
    [
        @body
        @to cell
    ]
}

* Run the body once if the cell holds anything, and not at all if it does not.
*
*     if cell:
*         body
*
* BrainFuck has no `if`. This is the idiom that stands in for one: ask the
* question into a flag, loop on the flag, and empty it so the loop runs once.
@macro when(cell, flag, scratch, spare, body) {
    @is_not_zero(cell, flag, scratch, spare)
    @to flag
    [
        @body
        @clear(flag)
    ]
}

* And the other way round.
*
*     if not cell:
*         body
@macro unless(cell, flag, scratch, spare, body) {
    @is_zero(cell, flag, scratch, spare)
    @to flag
    [
        @body
        @clear(flag)
    ]
}

* Print one character, through a cell set aside for it.
*
*     cell = letter
*     print(cell)
@macro say(cell, letter) {
    @put(cell, letter)
    .
}

@macro newline(cell) {
    @say(cell, NL)
}

* Print a value 0-9 as the digit it names, and leave it where it was. Adding
* the 48 and taking it back is cheaper than copying it somewhere to destroy.
*
*     print(chr(cell + ord('0')))
@macro show_digit(cell) {
    @to cell
    +{48}
    .
    @to cell
    -{48}
}
